00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:55.330
Hey, Steve.

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:56.335
STEVE MCGHEE: Hey, guys.

00:00:56.335 --> 00:00:56.960
How's it going?

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.002
CARTER MORGAN: Hi, Steve.

00:00:58.002 --> 00:00:59.230
Glad to have you here.

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:02.306
Can you tell us a little bit
about yourself and who you are?

00:01:02.306 --> 00:01:03.650
STEVE MCGHEE: Sure.

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:04.819
I'm Steve McGhee.

00:01:04.819 --> 00:01:08.990
I was an SRE in Google for
about 10 years, and then I left,

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:11.090
and I became a
customer of cloud.

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:13.208
And I learned how
to cloud, and how

00:01:13.208 --> 00:01:15.500
to use all the different
clouds, and just how difficult

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:21.150
it is actually to make
decisions and stuff like that.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.900
And then I came back to
Google to help more customers

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:24.890
do exactly that.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:28.400
I tend to focus on reliability,
because my background was

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.373
in site reliability engineering.

00:01:30.373 --> 00:01:31.790
CARTER MORGAN:
See, this is great.

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:33.560
Because something I'm
trying to figure out

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.635
is, people that are focused on
reliability, you would think

00:01:37.635 --> 00:01:39.260
that-- or at least
I would think that--

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.760
and Brian has said that I might
be a little misguided here--

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.600
that just having one
piece of hardware

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.700
that you upgrade and put
a lot of resources into

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.242
is going to be a lot
more reliable than having

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:51.200
lots of smaller parts
that have to communicate,

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.970
that can all fail.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:54.880
So where am I going wrong here?

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:55.790
STEVE MCGHEE: Yeah.

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:57.920
I mean, your intent is correct.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.660
It would be sweet if we just had
an infinitely dense, infinitely

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:04.355
fast computer.

00:02:04.355 --> 00:02:08.449
When you work on a system on
your laptop, the whole phrase,

00:02:08.449 --> 00:02:11.390
"it works on my machine,
and what's the problem?"

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:13.693
When we go into production,
we're scaling up.

00:02:13.693 --> 00:02:15.110
And we have to
scale up because we

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:17.450
don't want to have a
service for one person.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:19.760
We hopefully want
it for many people,

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.320
potentially even around the
world or something like that.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.010
So being able to scale
up into production

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:28.760
means that we run into
actual laws of physics.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:32.330
So speed of light comes into
play, density of material.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.640
You can't go so dense that
everything catches fire,

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:35.250
for example.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.140
That would be bad.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.070
And so that means we have to
start spreading things out.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:42.620
What it really
means is that we're

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.990
taking what was one computer,
and we're spreading it

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:49.590
across time and space, and we're
using many computers instead.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:55.580
So I hope that helps understand
what the basic problem is here.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.020
CARTER MORGAN: It really does.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.660
Because we've talked
about this a lot.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:01.520
Brian hammered home
a point last season.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:04.880
He said, a cloud VM is
a slice of a data center

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.730
more so than it was a slice
of one specific machine.

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:10.070
And so it seems like
that's playing out here.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:14.060
But what I don't understand is,
Google touts its reliability

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.190
and being able to be
up for a long time.

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:18.410
But how is it able to do
that with so many parts

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:20.368
that are constantly going
to fail, like a disk?

00:03:20.368 --> 00:03:25.210
STEVE MCGHEE: If you think
about what it used to be like,

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:28.010
during the dot-com
boom, we used to call

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:29.870
these gold-plated servers.

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:33.740
We would make the most
expensive solid server

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:37.100
that you could ever have with
many nicks, and many power

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.487
supplies, and many
everythings, and hot swappable

00:03:39.487 --> 00:03:40.070
this and that.

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:42.320
And those were pretty awesome.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:45.240
They lasted really, really well.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.180
But they were super expensive.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:50.060
And it turns out that
just putting all your eggs

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:53.120
into the basket, making the
one machine really reliable,

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.720
only gets you a little
bit of the return.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.050
Because you still have to have
some downtime for maintenance,

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:02.550
and scaling up, and
things like that.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.850
And so what we found
inside of Google

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:07.640
is that we found
we actually wanted

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:11.360
to horizontally scale,
which is adding more

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:16.850
machines at a time in order
to achieve planet-wide scale,

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:20.160
like have billions of
users, for example.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.070
You can't fit billions of users
on one very expensive machine.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.520
So once you start
horizontally scaling,

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:30.350
using the most expensive machine
possible with marginal return

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:31.650
doesn't really make sense.

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:36.530
And so instead of having
a really solid base,

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:39.410
we introduced resilience at
many layers in the stack.

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:44.240
And it turns out that is
way better for the service.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.160
It's more economical,
and it actually

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:49.313
allows you to move faster, which
sounds crazy, but it's true.

00:04:49.313 --> 00:04:51.230
BRIAN DORSEY: This shows
up in a lot of places

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:53.030
in Google Cloud around the VMs.

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:56.090
The VMs themselves are
insulated to some degree

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:58.910
from the underlying systems
that are live migration.

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:02.060
We've got different zones
of separation between them.

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:06.110
We have load balancers so
you can hit different pieces.

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:10.010
But it feels like there's a
core principle here or two.

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:13.150
I don't know if you could talk
a little bit more about that.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:14.690
STEVE MCGHEE: Yeah.

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:17.870
So I went to school
in computer science,

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:20.510
so I consider myself a
computer scientist, I guess,

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:21.500
or a software engineer.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:24.110
And I think everyone who's
super into computer science

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:28.070
knows the one holy thing
is layers of abstraction.

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:29.910
Abstraction solves everything.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:32.210
So really this is what
we're talking about here,

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:35.120
is that we're going to have
many layers of abstraction.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.420
And I jokingly refer to
this as like the matryoshka

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:42.695
doll, which is those little
nesting dolls you've seen.

00:05:42.695 --> 00:05:45.980
At the very middle, the tiny
little doll is your CPU.

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:47.810
And then it goes
up to a machine,

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:51.050
and it has a VM around
it, and then in a rack.

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:54.320
And it's in a data center,
and then the data center

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.220
is part of a zone, which
is then part of a region.

00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:00.570
And so at every layer
of this stack, if you're

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:06.030
able to perform some resilience
engineering to make sure

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:09.100
that you can take advantage
of that layer of the stack,

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:10.380
this is where you get really--

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:11.797
it's like
defense-in-depth, right?

00:06:11.797 --> 00:06:14.220
So you're able to
handle failures

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:15.690
at any level of this stack.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:17.160
Because disks fail.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:19.140
CPUs can go out.

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.990
Solar flares can cause
memory corruption.

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:28.013
Someone can cut a fiber
line to a data center,

00:06:28.013 --> 00:06:30.180
and the whole building could
potentially go offline.

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:31.410
We have floods happen.

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:32.430
I mean, they're rare.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:33.150
Fires happen.

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:34.530
They're rare, too.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:37.032
So there's lots of
potential failure modes

00:06:37.032 --> 00:06:38.490
that you have to
consider, and they

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:40.120
happen at many of these levels.

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:43.110
So being aware of all those
and having mitigations ready

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:44.495
for them is super important.

00:06:44.495 --> 00:06:45.090
BRIAN DORSEY: It's funny.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:46.890
We're calling it virtual
machines in the cloud,

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:49.057
but it's really virtual
machines, and virtual disks,

00:06:49.057 --> 00:06:50.565
and virtual load balancers and--

00:06:50.565 --> 00:06:51.690
STEVE MCGHEE: That's right.

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:53.910
I really liked what you said
in the previous episode,

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:55.785
that you're getting a
slice of a data center.

00:06:55.785 --> 00:06:58.320
Because we're able to take
all these component parts

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:03.000
and present them to a customer
as this nice abstraction.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.760
Like, it's this
computer, with this disk,

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:05.760
and it has this network.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:09.690
And in actuality, it might
be, the one disk you have

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:11.040
is actually three disks.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:13.350
But we're not really
showing you all three disks.

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:17.340
It's just like magically
giving this resilience

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:18.360
and redundancy to you.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:19.193
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah.

00:07:19.193 --> 00:07:22.410
I see how being able
to abstract away

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:24.130
the layer underneath
yourself and saying,

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:26.340
I don't care exactly which
zone is under this region.

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:28.320
As long as one of
them is up, I'm fine.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:29.403
That actually makes sense.

00:07:29.403 --> 00:07:30.820
And it makes sense
why we wouldn't

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:31.800
want one supercomputer.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:33.760
Because it'd be hard to do that.

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:35.670
But then now you have
to start managing

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:40.080
all these separate zones and VMs
and all this extra complexity

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:43.560
that comes in, which is one of
the downsides of abstraction

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:44.820
sometimes.

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:47.421
How does Google handle that?

00:07:47.421 --> 00:07:49.290
STEVE MCGHEE: A good question.

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:51.790
I work with a lot of companies
that ask exactly this.

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:52.530
They say, great.

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:55.072
You want us to spread all of
our computers around the planet?

00:07:55.072 --> 00:07:57.412
That doesn't sound like a
pain in the butt at all.

00:07:57.412 --> 00:07:59.370
You know, it sounds like,
well, how in the heck

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:02.050
do you expect me
to deal with that?

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:05.310
And again, the answer
comes back to abstraction.

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:08.010
So when you think about
it, you have one VM,

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:10.862
and the first step you're
going to get is many VMs.

00:08:10.862 --> 00:08:12.570
So we put these
together, and we put them

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:13.680
behind a load balancer.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:15.312
We call this a service.

00:08:15.312 --> 00:08:16.770
If you're familiar
with Kubernetes,

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:19.620
it's pretty much the same idea,
where you have a bunch of VMs,

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:23.100
and you have an entry point
that talks to all of them.

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:24.870
If you can then
take this service

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:27.510
and you can run it
across multiple zones,

00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:29.670
now we can call this
a regional service.

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:32.610
Because it now lives across many
of these zones in one region.

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:34.860
And then if you can get
that regional service

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:38.760
in many regions, I call
this a distributed service.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:41.760
There's no canonical
name for that right now.

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:44.370
But the idea is that it's
many of these services,

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:46.560
but they all do the same
thing, and they represent

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:49.270
the same business need.

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:53.290
And so you're able to now handle
regional level events as well.

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:54.420
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:55.710
I'm putting all this together.

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:57.930
I'm trying to really
piece this together.

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:01.440
Because it's still
hard to me to think,

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:03.102
these things can
go down so often,

00:09:03.102 --> 00:09:04.560
and that gives you
more reliability

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:06.660
because you're layering
on top of each other.

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:10.680
Maybe Brian-- you're always good
at giving me concrete examples.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:13.370
Where do we use this
in Google Cloud?

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:16.410
BRIAN DORSEY: So I think
the most common example

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:20.117
is when you have multiple VMs
that are doing the same job.

00:09:20.117 --> 00:09:22.200
Maybe they're a web server
or something like that.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:24.210
And you put a load
balancer in front of them.

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:28.020
And this shows up in
physical installations,

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:29.940
and it shows up in the cloud.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:32.730
It's maybe the most
common example of this.

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:34.440
So you have one
endpoint, and you

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:37.620
have multiple different back
ends that can do the work.

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:40.480
And if one of them goes
away, the work still happens.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:43.470
So I think that's my
favorite specific example.

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:48.650
But Steve, is there a more
general principle at work here?

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:50.190
STEVE MCGHEE: Yeah, totally.

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:53.880
So an analogy to think about
is, just think about boats

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:56.970
in the water, physical boats.

00:09:56.970 --> 00:10:00.030
If you poke a hole in the
bottom of your dinghy,

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:01.725
you're going to have
a bad day, right?

00:10:01.725 --> 00:10:03.160
That's no good.

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:05.100
So we call this "one
failure domain."

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:06.960
Your little dinghy
is a failure domain.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:10.080
Because there's only
one floor to it.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:12.157
And if you put a hole
anywhere in that floor,

00:10:12.157 --> 00:10:12.990
it's going to flood.

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:14.430
That's not great.

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:15.930
But then now think
of a bigger ship.

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:20.100
Think of a container ship or
some giant vessel of some kind.

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:22.305
They're like a bunch
of little boats

00:10:22.305 --> 00:10:24.930
tied together, because they have
these things called bulkheads.

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:27.540
So I wouldn't do
this, but you could

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:31.980
poke one hole in the bottom
of one of these big ships,

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:32.910
and it would be fine.

00:10:32.910 --> 00:10:35.410
Because what it would do is,
it would fill up that bulkhead,

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:38.460
but the rest of the boat is so
buoyant that it stays floating.

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:41.040
So essentially, you're
taking these things that

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.890
could fail with a hole, and
you stick them together,

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:46.650
and now the system as
a whole doesn't fail

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:49.717
with that same failure mode.

00:10:49.717 --> 00:10:51.300
BRIAN DORSEY: So
that's exactly what's

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:53.550
going on with our VMs and
load balancer kind of thing.

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:01.590
How does this work?

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:03.880
So if you've got VMs,
and load balancers,

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:07.720
and these different bits--
and you mentioned zones,

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:11.490
and regions, and
distributive services--

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:12.970
how do we reason about this?

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:16.450
How do we decide
how much complexity

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:19.322
we're going to put into this?

00:11:19.322 --> 00:11:21.330
STEVE MCGHEE: A good question.

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:24.310
Yeah, that was kind of the
abstract, academic answer.

00:11:24.310 --> 00:11:27.300
And so now let's talk
about reality, Steve.

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:30.225
This is an important
thing, to understand

00:11:30.225 --> 00:11:34.240
what level of
availability you can

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:36.430
expect from the systems
you're building on top of.

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:39.370
How often do we get a hole
in the bottom of a boat?

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:40.400
Can we get a number?

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:41.650
That would be nice.

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:44.740
The way that we suggest
people think about it

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:47.875
is that, for something
that lives in one zone,

00:11:47.875 --> 00:11:51.627
we say it's going to be
available 99.9% of the time.

00:11:51.627 --> 00:11:53.710
Specifically, we say its
design is to be available

00:11:53.710 --> 00:11:55.780
99.9% of the time.

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:58.480
That basically means
it's going to be

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:02.240
down or unavailable
for 40 minutes a month.

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:03.940
And it's important
to think that's not

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:07.243
the best-case scenario or
the worst-case scenario.

00:12:07.243 --> 00:12:09.160
That's something you can
pretty much count on.

00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:11.680
So it's pretty much
going to happen.

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:13.450
Similarly, if you
have something that

00:12:13.450 --> 00:12:16.660
is across many
zones in a region,

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:18.340
we call that four nines.

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:20.860
It's 99.99% available.

00:12:20.860 --> 00:12:22.580
And the way to
think about that is,

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:25.790
it's 40 minutes a
year of downtime,

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:27.970
which is actually pretty good.

00:12:27.970 --> 00:12:30.280
Computers stay up quite a lot.

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:32.973
40 minutes in an entire
year, it's a tiny--

00:12:32.973 --> 00:12:35.140
I mean, we know exactly how
big of a fraction it is.

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:38.020
But sometimes maybe that's
still not acceptable for you.

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:39.580
But it's up to what
it is that you're

00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:40.705
putting in these computers.

00:12:40.705 --> 00:12:44.050
Are you OK with those three
nines or those four nines?

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:48.160
And you have to make a decision
on what's appropriate for you.

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:50.410
CARTER MORGAN: I get this
now because basically you're

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:55.450
saying, OK, I have two computers
that are only down 40 minutes

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:56.187
every year.

00:12:56.187 --> 00:12:58.270
If we layer them over each
other, when one's down,

00:12:58.270 --> 00:12:59.410
the other one's not down.

00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:01.000
We're good.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:03.740
And so it's just
interesting seeing

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:07.510
all of this that comes in that
you have to manage on your own.

00:13:07.510 --> 00:13:10.510
I wonder if I have to come in
as the developer and be like,

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:12.010
OK, I have to
schedule this computer

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:15.160
to be up watching for
the other one to be down,

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:17.200
or is this something that
the infrastructure can

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:18.610
start to take care of for you?

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:19.870
STEVE MCGHEE: Yeah.

00:13:19.870 --> 00:13:22.720
The good news is that
this is a problem

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:25.240
that, inside of Google
for the past 20 years,

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:26.240
we've been dealing with.

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:28.698
And so we've come up with a
bunch of solutions, basically--

00:13:28.698 --> 00:13:30.430
a bunch of improvements
to the system.

00:13:30.430 --> 00:13:33.130
And we're bringing those
to Cloud customers.

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:35.590
And so for example, we
know that sometimes you

00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:38.440
have to power down a rack
of machines or something

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:39.010
like that.

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:44.230
And if your server is on
that rack, what do you do?

00:13:44.230 --> 00:13:45.130
That's a real bummer.

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:47.540
Should we tell you ahead of
time or something like that?

00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:50.890
But instead, we built this thing
called live migration, which

00:13:50.890 --> 00:13:54.100
basically takes the soul of your
machine, the running system,

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:56.350
the running software,
and it transports it

00:13:56.350 --> 00:13:59.590
magically across the room or
whatever to another machine.

00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:01.942
And that way, we can safely
power down this rack,

00:14:01.942 --> 00:14:02.650
bring it back up.

00:14:02.650 --> 00:14:04.900
And maybe we had to replace
a fuse or something,

00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:06.640
I'm not really sure.

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:09.940
And basically, it's one less
thing for you to worry about.

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:12.250
And when I said
before that machines

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:15.880
are designed to have
99.9% of availability,

00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:17.800
it accounts for stuff like this.

00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:21.982
So this is how we're giving
you 99.9% availability.

00:14:21.982 --> 00:14:23.440
There's a bunch of
other stuff too.

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:25.607
Sometimes the building we
need to do maintenance on,

00:14:25.607 --> 00:14:28.600
or maybe the machine
itself, has a problem.

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:29.240
Or who knows?

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:31.240
There's all kinds of
things that could go wrong.

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:33.560
And we aggregate all that
risk into one number,

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:35.770
and then we give you
that expectation.

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:37.598
And you basically
don't have to worry

00:14:37.598 --> 00:14:39.640
about all those weird
things that could go wrong.

00:14:39.640 --> 00:14:42.430
You just know this machine
is going to be up 99.9%

00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:43.000
of the time.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:46.120
BRIAN DORSEY: So there's a
whole bunch of these edge cases,

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:48.340
I guess, if you
will, that are just

00:14:48.340 --> 00:14:51.550
handled because you're
running a VM in a cloud,

00:14:51.550 --> 00:14:56.540
instead of on an individual
physical machine.

00:14:56.540 --> 00:14:58.930
So we've got all of those.

00:14:58.930 --> 00:15:02.260
We've got these tools.

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:04.630
If something similar is
happening at the disk level,

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:07.270
we've got load balancers
that are built out

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:10.450
of lots of machines, kind
of data center-level things.

00:15:10.450 --> 00:15:13.090
We've got groups of VMs,
like managed instance

00:15:13.090 --> 00:15:14.590
groups, and then
zones, and regions,

00:15:14.590 --> 00:15:17.060
and all this kind of stuff.

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:21.940
But then I don't know.

00:15:21.940 --> 00:15:26.698
Some of it is to the app
level problem, right?

00:15:26.698 --> 00:15:28.240
CARTER MORGAN: You're
saying you want

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:29.740
to design an
application that can

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:32.515
handle this 40 minutes of
downtime in year two as well?

00:15:35.358 --> 00:15:36.150
That's interesting.

00:15:36.150 --> 00:15:36.942
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:15:36.942 --> 00:15:39.630
OK, so we get to the point
where we can trust the VMs

00:15:39.630 --> 00:15:43.216
and then build from
there, I guess.

00:15:43.216 --> 00:15:44.240
STEVE MCGHEE: Yeah.

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:47.840
The important point is to
know what level of trust

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:49.070
you can put into the VM.

00:15:49.070 --> 00:15:51.140
And what you're really
doing is, you're

00:15:51.140 --> 00:15:53.690
putting that trust into the
hands of your cloud provider.

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:57.380
And you're saying, look, just
tell me what it's going to be.

00:15:57.380 --> 00:15:59.960
And on GCP, for our
VMs, we tell you,

00:15:59.960 --> 00:16:03.890
it's going to be 99.9
for one VM in one zone.

00:16:03.890 --> 00:16:06.390
And then it just means that you
get to put that all that out

00:16:06.390 --> 00:16:09.830
of your mind, and you
can trust that number

00:16:09.830 --> 00:16:10.922
is going to be true.

00:16:10.922 --> 00:16:12.380
And now you have
to work around it.

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:15.860
It's important that you don't
think that that number is close

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:17.990
enough to 100-- let's
just say it's 100%--

00:16:17.990 --> 00:16:19.430
because that's not true.

00:16:19.430 --> 00:16:22.040
If you make a distinction
between 99.9 and 100,

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:24.740
you're going to make actually
radically different design

00:16:24.740 --> 00:16:26.648
choices in your application.

00:16:26.648 --> 00:16:28.940
You're going to start to
introduce things like retries,

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:31.400
or you're going to introduce
things like checkpointing,

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:34.970
or you're going to allow your
service to potentially live

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:36.290
in two places at the same time.

00:16:36.290 --> 00:16:37.610
What if one request
comes in here,

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:39.710
and one request comes in
there, and this one's up,

00:16:39.710 --> 00:16:41.450
and this one's not?

00:16:41.450 --> 00:16:43.700
Now that you've allowed
a little bit of failure

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:45.795
into your understanding
of the model,

00:16:45.795 --> 00:16:48.170
you change the way you think
about designing your system.

00:16:48.170 --> 00:16:51.740
And to me, that was one of
the fundamental things that

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:54.200
made Google succeed
from the very beginning,

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:58.370
was that we designed our systems
to allow some form of failure.

00:16:58.370 --> 00:17:01.463
And then we just added that
resiliency over and over

00:17:01.463 --> 00:17:03.380
and over again, all the
way through the stack.

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:05.060
CARTER MORGAN: This
is so interesting.

00:17:05.060 --> 00:17:07.310
Because the way you just
worded that, it finally

00:17:07.310 --> 00:17:09.140
made it click for me.

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:11.000
By planning for
failure, it doesn't

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:12.603
matter what the
failure is, because I

00:17:12.603 --> 00:17:13.520
have the case covered.

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:15.859
It's going to be some delay to
getting this information to me

00:17:15.859 --> 00:17:16.470
in some way.

00:17:16.470 --> 00:17:17.359
So I'll retry it.

00:17:17.359 --> 00:17:18.890
I'll do something like that.

00:17:18.890 --> 00:17:21.589
And then that allows you
to build more resilience

00:17:21.589 --> 00:17:23.270
and reliability.

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:25.700
And again, it's an abstraction
away from the core thing.

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:26.825
I don't care why it's that.

00:17:26.825 --> 00:17:27.793
Let me abstract.

00:17:27.793 --> 00:17:29.210
To put it in the
real world, there

00:17:29.210 --> 00:17:31.210
are some important use
cases where this matters.

00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:34.541
And that's why there's so much
effort going into this thought.

00:17:34.541 --> 00:17:35.810
STEVE MCGHEE: That's right.

00:17:35.810 --> 00:17:39.650
It's important to not
try to get the highest

00:17:39.650 --> 00:17:41.870
level of reliability out
of absolutely everything.

00:17:41.870 --> 00:17:46.070
Because you're going to have
some websites, or services,

00:17:46.070 --> 00:17:48.770
or whatever that can be down.

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:50.270
It's no big deal.

00:17:50.270 --> 00:17:51.260
It's not a problem.

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:53.240
But there are other
services which

00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:55.230
are actually really important.

00:17:55.230 --> 00:17:59.300
So let's say there's a service
out there that responds when

00:17:59.300 --> 00:18:02.540
someone needs to get a new
delivery of oxygen tanks

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:06.170
to some medical center
or something like that.

00:18:06.170 --> 00:18:08.120
We should probably
make sure that that one

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:10.320
works most of the time.

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:14.390
And even if you make a request
and it doesn't work right away,

00:18:14.390 --> 00:18:17.930
but we know that once we
put it in, it was received,

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:20.900
and someone will take action
on it or something like that--

00:18:20.900 --> 00:18:23.133
this is a silly
example, but there

00:18:23.133 --> 00:18:24.800
are more and more of
these services that

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:27.110
are becoming more and more
important to the world.

00:18:27.110 --> 00:18:28.568
Because frankly,
we're just putting

00:18:28.568 --> 00:18:31.430
more of our society online.

00:18:31.430 --> 00:18:34.160
So I think COVID is
actually an interesting time

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:37.250
to go through this whole
reliability effort.

00:18:37.250 --> 00:18:39.890
Because we saw a lot
of services became

00:18:39.890 --> 00:18:43.490
more and more important
to individuals

00:18:43.490 --> 00:18:44.787
in an accelerated kind of way.

00:18:44.787 --> 00:18:46.370
It was already getting
more important.

00:18:46.370 --> 00:18:49.440
But during the last couple of
years, it went really steep.

00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:52.400
So think about video
conferencing for school kids,

00:18:52.400 --> 00:18:55.850
or just ordering
things from home.

00:18:55.850 --> 00:18:59.040
Being able to do this online
is becoming more important.

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:03.020
So making sure that you can
do it when you want to do it

00:19:03.020 --> 00:19:06.632
is more of a big deal
even than it used to be.

00:19:06.632 --> 00:19:09.220
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah,
things getting very real.

00:19:09.220 --> 00:19:10.630
More of our life goes online.

00:19:10.630 --> 00:19:13.550
It matters more and is
more and more critical.

00:19:13.550 --> 00:19:15.110
So OK, this has been amazing.

00:19:15.110 --> 00:19:17.110
I think we're going to
have to wrap up this one.

00:19:17.110 --> 00:19:20.630
But I feel like there's a
lot more to talk about here.

00:19:20.630 --> 00:19:23.740
And so Steve, would you be up
for coming back and talking

00:19:23.740 --> 00:19:27.190
specifically about
what questions

00:19:27.190 --> 00:19:29.740
you ask about
whether you're going

00:19:29.740 --> 00:19:32.700
to try to get another nine, and
then how do you actually do it?

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:34.450
STEVE MCGHEE: Yeah, I
think I can make it.

00:19:34.450 --> 00:19:35.782
That should be fine.

00:19:35.782 --> 00:19:37.240
I think this is an
important thing.

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:39.200
People really want to
hear about this stuff.

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:40.390
So I'd love to come back.

00:19:40.390 --> 00:19:41.920
CARTER MORGAN: Hey, well,
thank you so much, Steve.

00:19:41.920 --> 00:19:43.180
Brian, thank you so much.

00:19:43.180 --> 00:19:45.742
If you're listening at home,
please write in the comments

00:19:45.742 --> 00:19:47.950
if there's anything you've
learned about reliability.

00:19:47.950 --> 00:19:50.320
I know I learned a few
things, maybe you did, too.

00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:50.950
Thank you.

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:54.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

